I might have a bathroom break or something.

20
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.950
I won't be for the whole thing, but I'll be in and out.

21
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.210
We can provide you with a bathroom break.

22
00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:48.260
Okay, good.

23
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:48.750
That'd be good.

24
00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.110
That'd be good.

25
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.010
We can take a bathroom break.

26
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:50.610
I'm old school.

27
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.670
Well, I'm old, so that's part of it, you know.

28
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.650
I need everybody to know that I've already teared up twice since Creed has been here.

29
00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:58.070
Oh.

30
00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.690
You've said two touching – well, first of all, just seeing you almost burst into tears.

31
00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:06.070
But then you've said, like, five amazing things that have touched my heart already.

32
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.110
Well, as soon as I get together with you, it's just back – we're back on the set,

33
00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.330
you know, and it's all like, oh, I miss you girls.

34
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.270
Creed lives down the street from me.

35
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:15.460
Yes.

36
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.490
I think everybody who knows me knows this.

37
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:17.670
The world knows that.

38
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.810
Don't give him my address, please.

39
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:19.290
I won't.

40
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:19.530
Okay.

41
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.450
Creed's address is – his hidden key is –

42
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.010
Yes, yes.

43
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.390
Yes, we are neighbors, and it's one of the joys of my life that we're neighbors.

44
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.770
I love that you're right around the corner.

45
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.110
I love that we have –

46
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.090
We see each other walking.

47
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.070
We see each other walking.

48
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:33.670
We see each other hiking around the street.

49
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:34.030
We do.

50
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:34.510
Hey!

51
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.470
You were walking – was it your daughter's dog?

52
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.070
Your son's dog?

53
00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.210
My son's a little Yorkie, yeah.

54
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.030
A little Yorkie, and I hear, hey, pumpkin, and I turn around and it's Creed.

55
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:46.990
And we've also maybe had some adult beverage and red solo cups.

56
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:47.340
We may have.

57
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:47.770
We may have.

58
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.890
And we like to do a little bit of what we call the sauce trail and walk around having a glass of wine.

59
00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:54.050
And watching documentaries.

60
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:58.970
Oh, yeah, Creed loves – anything where, like, animals go crazy, like planet Earth.

61
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.170
Yes, all that stuff.

62
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:00.750
I just love it.

63
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:01.110
All right.

64
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.850
Well, we are really excited to have you here.

65
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.110
We are discussing the Halloween episode.

66
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:05.790
Yes.

67
00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:06.710
Right, right.

68
00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.530
Season 2, Episode 5.

69
00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:15.350
This was written by Greg Daniels and directed by Paul Feig, and it is the first episode that Creed speaks.

70
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.810
Well, I actually spoke in Diversity Day.

71
00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.550
In Diversity Day, I had lines with Phyllis.

72
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:22.510
Yes, I told her.

73
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.030
She paid me afterwards, said, well, the first A.D., remember that girl we had the first A.D.?

74
00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:27.230
Yeah.

75
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:27.830
The first season?

76
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.550
She thought I was part of the cast at the time.

77
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.230
So she came and we had little hands, and she said, now, you and Phyllis just start talking.

78
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.150
And Phyllis said, wait.

79
00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:35.950
I said, never mind.

80
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:36.710
It's okay.

81
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:38.830
Never mind.

82
00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.510
Phyllis was going to out you.

83
00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:41.830
Phyllis was going to out me.

84
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:42.510
So I said, never mind.

85
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:42.970
It's fine.

86
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.970
And so we started – and I started talking about, you know, sacrificing goats or something, you know.

87
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:48.530
Of course you did.

88
00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.310
Trying to tell her what was on her head.

89
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.190
Your improv was sacrificing goats.

90
00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.010
Yes, yes, yes.

91
00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:54.670
So you can hear me in the background.

92
00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.390
And later on, they said, wait a minute, Creed's talking in the background.

93
00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.950
And I said, oh, shoot, how did that happen?

94
00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.650
Yeah, how'd that happen, Creed?

95
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:06.230
And so they paid me on the side, but they didn't say, but we can't – I said, no, it's fine.

96
00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:06.950
It's fine.

97
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.370
And then off camera, then I also was the voice for the ride.

98
00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.050
Yes, for the mining ride.

99
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.290
For the mining ride, you know.

100
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.090
You were so funny.

101
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.910
I think they paid attention to that one.

102
00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.150
Yeah, you were so funny as the voice of the mining ride.

103
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.110
And Isabel was watching that episode with me, and she said, mom, I know that voice.

104
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.090
I know that voice.

105
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.330
And I was like, wait, you're – oh, my gosh.

106
00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.390
She was like, I think it's Creed.

107
00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:29.290
She?

108
00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:29.990
Oh, yeah.

109
00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:30.870
How discerning is she?

110
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.790
She is – I mean, I didn't catch it.

111
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.210
And then I looked it up, and I'm like, Isabel, we do know that voice.

112
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:35.730
It's Creed.

113
00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:41.990
Yeah, but anyway, this is the first time where I actually had some serious lines.

114
00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.710
It was six and a half pages.

115
00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.170
I remember Ken Zabornik threw the script down.

116
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.290
He said, okay, here it is.

117
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.790
This is your moment.

118
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:50.210
Wow.

119
00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.510
And I had memorized it forwards and backwards.

120
00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:58.870
I had set a timer on a little recorder at a night, subliminally at two and three in the morning,

121
00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.970
that would go off and play the lines while I was sleeping.

122
00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:02.710
Creep.

123
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.150
I had it backwards and forwards.

124
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:04.550
No joke.

125
00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:05.110
I had it backwards.

126
00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:05.170
Wow.

127
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.030
I knew this was the Rubicon.

128
00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:08.790
This is the place that I was going to get.

129
00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.450
My intuition said, this is it.

130
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:16.490
And then, of course, we walk in the office, and Greg, they came in and said, by the way,

131
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.930
we've made changes, of course, to the lines, and we've reversed them around and added some

132
00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:21.290
new stuff.

133
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.590
You're okay, Steve goes, yeah, fine.

134
00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:23.430
I go, oh.

135
00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.270
Because they used to do that to us all the time.

136
00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:26.690
Oh, all the time.

137
00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.870
We would come in, and you'd just get new pages and then shoot the scene five minutes later.

138
00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.530
Yeah, they would hand you the pages, and sometimes, we've talked about this,

139
00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:34.550
the pages would be hot.

140
00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.250
Right off the copier.

141
00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.150
Right off the copier.

142
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.030
And we'd drop them some.

143
00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:38.210
Yeah.

144
00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:39.430
Ow, ow, ow, ow.

145
00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:45.750
Now change your lines that you have learned, and you've been having a tape recorder play

146
00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.030
at three in the morning to you.

147
00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:52.350
Do you remember the backdrop there right off of where my desk and Kate's desk was?

148
00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.450
And there was the screen there.

149
00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.790
And you could sneak around the back and get to the green room from behind it.

150
00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:57.330
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

151
00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:58.450
That's set up like that.

152
00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.450
I walked back like that after they told me this and put my hands up and said, okay, here

153
00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:02.810
it is.

154
00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.250
And I said, I've come this far.

155
00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.330
I'm going to do this.

156
00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:10.070
And once we started, though, with Steve, it was like end of battle, you know?

157
00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:10.610
Yeah.

158
00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:15.530
And there was some improvisation on that scene because I was paraphrasing my lines because

159
00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.190
I was desperate.

160
00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.330
Well, you knocked it out of the park, Creed.

161
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.350
Yeah, yeah.

162
00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.110
Well, Creed, you started out as a musician.

163
00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:24.510
Yeah.

164
00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.110
And a very long, successful career as a musician.

165
00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:32.990
How did you even end up on the show in the first place as our stand-in slash background

166
00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:33.450
person?

167
00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:33.950
Background person.

168
00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:39.490
I was working on Bernie Mac, and Ken Kwapis came on to direct an episode.

169
00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:44.630
And they'd been using me for all these little vignettes where I was doing funny.

170
00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.370
And if you ever watch old Bernie Mac, you'll see me all the time in the background mugging

171
00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:49.630
up.

172
00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.830
I really want to see that.

173
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:53.350
I want to go back.

174
00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.770
I'm always in the background doing some stupid stuff, but they just allow me because I made

175
00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:57.370
Bernie laugh.

176
00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:58.690
I made him laugh.

177
00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:05.490
And so Ken was talking about this guy's funny, and he heard from Joe Moore, the first AD that

178
00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.550
I was in the grassroots.

179
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.130
So he went, he sent to Amoeba for some albums for me to sign because he was a big grassroots

180
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:11.550
fan.

181
00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.930
And we started talking and talked about music, and he's very knowledgeable.

182
00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:21.850
Then I found out that he was doing the Office of American Workplace, and I was a big fan

183
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:22.850
of the Ricky Gervais show.

184
00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.550
So he gave me his number.

185
00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:28.510
Now, I've never, ever called anybody like that before.

186
00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.270
But I called him as my intuition said, this feels right.

187
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:32.890
This feels right.

188
00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.710
And Ken would not have given you his number.

189
00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.530
I feel like Ken is so discerning.

190
00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:37.070
Oh, my God.

191
00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:37.510
Yeah, he didn't.

192
00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.810
If he didn't mean, call me.

193
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:39.110
Yeah.

194
00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:39.550
Yeah.

195
00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:40.310
So he did.

196
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.270
And so he talked to Greg, and then I talked to Ken later, and he said, well, I talked to

197
00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:45.550
Greg.

198
00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.350
I told him you're a very interesting and funny guy.

199
00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:53.790
And he said, well, what we'll do, and this is his words, Greg's words, we'll put him

200
00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:57.810
in the background and see if we can work him into the mix in that first time.

201
00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:58.850
That was working in the mix.

202
00:06:58.930 --> 00:06:59.830
That's all I need to hear.

203
00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:07.670
Or that's why I took liberties just kind of like pretending that I was part of the group

204
00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.370
and that paid off, you know?

205
00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:09.970
Yes.

206
00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:11.350
Oh, I love that.

207
00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.130
Oh, that is so cool.

208
00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:12.910
That is awesome.

209
00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.090
What's next?

210
00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:15.090
Should we do a summary?

211
00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.450
Jenna, do you have one?

212
00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:17.170
You know I do.

213
00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:17.530
Yes.

214
00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:23.450
So in this episode, Halloween, Michael is told that he must fire someone during the month

215
00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.890
of October due to cutbacks at Dunder Mifflin.

216
00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:32.150
But in true Michael fashion, he waits until the very last day of October, which also happens

217
00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:32.910
to be Halloween.

218
00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.390
He also has no idea who to fire.

219
00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:36.470
He has not made a decision.

220
00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:41.650
He spends the entire episode trying to figure out what to do while the rest of us are dressed

221
00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.550
in our costumes for the Halloween party.

222
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.030
I love that.

223
00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:46.030
I love that.

224
00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:50.030
I love the juxtaposition of that someone is going to get fired while wearing a ridiculous

225
00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:50.550
costume.

226
00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:50.890
Yes.

227
00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.850
And they're going to have to go home fired in that outfit.

228
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.810
Well, I have a fast fact about this episode.

229
00:07:55.970 --> 00:08:01.470
Fast fact number one, Greg Daniels wrote this episode, and he told me that the inspiration

230
00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:07.170
for this episode was that he had always wanted to do a story about someone being fired, but

231
00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.150
from the point of view of the boss rather than the employee.

232
00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:15.250
He felt like we always see stories of people being fired and how they're wrestling with

233
00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.210
having been fired.

234
00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.030
But he was like, you never see what it does to the boss.

235
00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.390
That's the genius of Greg Daniels right there.

236
00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:21.870
Absolutely.

237
00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:26.930
Well, the problem that this plot presented for Greg was that he was going to have to

238
00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.190
fire someone on the show.

239
00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:32.230
He was going to have to pick a Dunder Mifflin employee to be part of the plot.

240
00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:32.650
Yes.

241
00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:37.450
Well, he couldn't fire any of the series regular characters because we all had contracts,

242
00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:42.830
and he didn't want to fire any of the supporting cast, even though at this point you guys still

243
00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.390
did not have your full-time contracts, right?

244
00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:45.930
No, we didn't.

245
00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.970
We didn't become series regulars till Booze Cruise.

246
00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.230
So listen, we were all on the chopping block.

247
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.330
Were you a little worried when you saw this episode?

248
00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:53.390
Well, maybe.

249
00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.030
Well, Greg didn't have any intention of losing them.

250
00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.610
He didn't want to lose anyone in the warehouse.

251
00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:03.850
But he also told me he didn't want to hire someone new that you'd never seen on the show

252
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:04.810
just to fire them.

253
00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.490
He felt like those stakes would feel very manufactured.

254
00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:13.270
So he turned to our two background actors, Creed and Devin.

